//! The init inside Tessera's system volume, in Kira.
//!
//! This is the program the machine belongs to once PID 1 in the initramfs has
//! mounted the system volume, made it the root, and executed what is here. It is
//! still PID 1 — the handover replaced the image, not the process — so everything
//! true of an init is true of it: returning is a kernel panic, and whatever the
//! machine reparents onto it is its to reap.
//!
//! What it does not do yet is take over the boot options. The kernel's command
//! line reaches the initramfs, and the handover passes no arguments on, so this
//! program cannot be told anything by whoever booted the machine. Powering off at
//! the end is that gap standing in for a decision: it makes a boot that reached
//! the system volume end on its own, the way `tessera.autoexit` does one stage
//! earlier, and it is the first thing to go when the handover learns to carry
//! arguments.

import Foundation
import LibTessera

/// Runs the system init: announces the boot, then powers off, idling if the platform refuses, and never returns.
@Main
function main() {
    print("")
    // The line a boot test greps for when it boots with a system volume.
    // Changing its wording means changing scripts/run-qemu.sh with it.
    print("tessera: system volume is the root, pid 1 is the system's own init")
    print("tessera: this init came off a read-only volume, not out of the initramfs")
    print("")

    // Only answered if the platform refuses to power off.
    match machineStop(.PowerOff) {
        Ok -> log fault("power off answered without powering off; idling instead")
        Error(failed) -> log fault(
            "power off was refused; idling instead",
            error: errorName(failed)
        )
    }
    while true {
        match threadSleep(Deadline.infinite) {
            Ok -> {}
            // Woken early; sleep again.
            Error(failed) -> absorb failed
        }
    }
}
